t makes sense where these  two cells decide what they're going to  become one becomes that anchor cell  which will invade  through the basement membrane later and  the other becomes a  ventral uterine cell and taylor has  preliminary data suggesting that this  fate specification decision is uh  tightly linked to the cell cycle  and as i mentioned before in the  beginning that anchor cell that we're  looking at which invades through the  basement membrane  must be held in g0 g1 in order to  undergo its invasive program  and now we're set up to uh really  look at the cell cycle state in those  anchor cells  and uh we know that perturbations that  prevent these cells from invading and  cause them to divide and enter the cell  cycle but now we can look more finely at  what specific cell cycle state these  cells are in  and what i'm particularly interested in  understanding  is um the regulation of the cell cycle  during cell migration  and so in c elegans there's this sex  myoblast cell  which provides a really nice model for  this so the cl against sex myolast or  the sm cell  is a migratory cell lineage and this sm  cell  is born um in a more posterior end of  the animal and then it will migrate 65  microns  to land at the center of the gonad and  this cell there's actually a  left right symmetry here so there's an  sm cell on either side of the worm  that migrates to the centers they flank  the gonad  each each cell will then undergo three  rounds at division  and they'll differentiate into the volvo  muscle and the uterine muscle cells  and so there'll be a total of 16 of  these cells in the animal  and these cells are required for  egg laying of the adult animal  and so we can visualize the sm cells um  here and we can see during the one cell  stage  and then they'll divide those three  rounds and then begin to terminally  differentiate  into the muscle cells  and so we can pair this sm cell uh  lineage tracer with our dhb cell cycle  state sensor and ask  what sta